\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Distributional differences are visible, but their value depends on the evaluator.} \textbf  {a}, The premise, drawn on one response coordinate: two drugs produce an identical mean shift, but under drug B the resistant minority does not move and the responding bulk overshoots to compensate, so the population mean is preserved while the two drugs do opposite things to the cells that matter. Schematic. \textbf  {b}, The inverse retrieval task and the two ways to score it: a distributional score keeps the whole candidate population, whereas mean-signature cosine collapses it to a single delta vector, and both return one ranked candidate list. \textbf  {c}, Two candidate populations placed on the same sample mean, with the marginal densities above the scatter showing that one is bimodal and the other unimodal: means tie, distributions separate. \textbf  {d}, When an evaluator is independent. A retrieval score graded by a metric of its own kind is one function evaluated once (coupled, Class A), so the ranking cannot lose; the same ranking sent to an outside judge can be confirmed or overturned (Class B/C). \textbf  {e}, The three evidence classes ordered by independence from the retrieval objective, with the headline metric of published method families (filled for Class A only) against what this study reports (all three, its Class-C readout imported). Its Class-C readout is an imported, semi-real functional oracle built from dose-response profiles in cell lines the transcriptional data never saw; a Class-C claim measured on the same cells would require wet-lab validation.}}{5}{figure.caption.1}\protected@file@percent }
\providecommand*\caption@xref[2]{\@setref\relax\@undefined{#1}}
\newlabel{fig:1}{{1}{5}{\textbf {Distributional differences are visible, but their value depends on the evaluator.} \textbf {a}, The premise, drawn on one response coordinate: two drugs produce an identical mean shift, but under drug B the resistant minority does not move and the responding bulk overshoots to compensate, so the population mean is preserved while the two drugs do opposite things to the cells that matter. Schematic. \textbf {b}, The inverse retrieval task and the two ways to score it: a distributional score keeps the whole candidate population, whereas mean-signature cosine collapses it to a single delta vector, and both return one ranked candidate list. \textbf {c}, Two candidate populations placed on the same sample mean, with the marginal densities above the scatter showing that one is bimodal and the other unimodal: means tie, distributions separate. \textbf {d}, When an evaluator is independent. A retrieval score graded by a metric of its own kind is one function evaluated once (coupled, Class A), so the ranking cannot lose; the same ranking sent to an outside judge can be confirmed or overturned (Class B/C). \textbf {e}, The three evidence classes ordered by independence from the retrieval objective, with the headline metric of published method families (filled for Class A only) against what this study reports (all three, its Class-C readout imported). Its Class-C readout is an imported, semi-real functional oracle built from dose-response profiles in cell lines the transcriptional data never saw; a Class-C claim measured on the same cells would require wet-lab validation}{figure.caption.1}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Mean and distributional retrieval form a continuous spectrum of variance sensitivity.} \textbf  {a}, Generative model $X=\mu +\lambda \epsilon $: the mean signature is the $\lambda \to 0$ limit. \textbf  {b}, Scaling only residual variance leaves the mean fixed. \textbf  {c}, Energy distance converges to the mean-distance floor as residual spread vanishes. \textbf  {d}, Under the implemented cosine kernel, mean cosine and CMap cosine are numerically identical (0.388); WTCS is a rank-based sibling. \textbf  {e}, The $\beta $-interpolation spectrum from mean-aggregation to worst-case, with energy equal to single-cluster coverage. \textbf  {f}, Metric-family hierarchy: every limit collapses to the mean.}}{7}{figure.caption.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:2}{{2}{7}{\textbf {Mean and distributional retrieval form a continuous spectrum of variance sensitivity.} \textbf {a}, Generative model $X=\mu +\lambda \epsilon $: the mean signature is the $\lambda \to 0$ limit. \textbf {b}, Scaling only residual variance leaves the mean fixed. \textbf {c}, Energy distance converges to the mean-distance floor as residual spread vanishes. \textbf {d}, Under the implemented cosine kernel, mean cosine and CMap cosine are numerically identical (0.388); WTCS is a rank-based sibling. \textbf {e}, The $\beta $-interpolation spectrum from mean-aggregation to worst-case, with energy equal to single-cluster coverage. \textbf {f}, Metric-family hierarchy: every limit collapses to the mean}{figure.caption.2}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Under objective-aligned metrics, distributional retrieval looks decisively stronger.} \textbf  {a}, Hit@1 ladder: energy 0.837 down to mean/CMap cosine 0.388, unweighted macro-mean over the seven (task $\times $ setting) cells of three retrieval tasks. \textbf  {b}, Per-task Hit@1. The advantage holds on the controlled and cross-line SciPlex3 tasks but \emph  {not} on Frangieh, the only natural real dataset in the macro-mean, where mean/CMap cosine (0.600) beats energy (0.578). The macro-mean hides this counterexample; we state it here and in the text. \textbf  {c}, Per-query regret reduction over \emph  {all} 765 partial-observed queries: median $+0.118$, 72\% improved (the $+0.119$ quoted elsewhere is the 621-query gate-recommended subset, and the two are indistinguishable, which is panel \textbf  {d}'s point). \textbf  {d}, Recommended ($+0.119$) versus non-recommended ($+0.122$) subsets: the gate does not concentrate the gain. \textbf  {e}, Alpha-crossover. The energy advantage does not close monotonically as the two subpopulations merge, and it does so in only one of the three cell lines (K562, from $1.00$ to $0.05$ in Hit@1 as $\alpha $ rises from $0.5$ to $0.9$); in A549 and MCF7 the gap is as large or larger at high $\alpha $. We report the per-line curves rather than a pooled trend for that reason. \textbf  {f}, All five distributional metrics show positive Class-A regret reduction.}}{9}{figure.caption.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:3}{{3}{9}{\textbf {Under objective-aligned metrics, distributional retrieval looks decisively stronger.} \textbf {a}, Hit@1 ladder: energy 0.837 down to mean/CMap cosine 0.388, unweighted macro-mean over the seven (task $\times $ setting) cells of three retrieval tasks. \textbf {b}, Per-task Hit@1. The advantage holds on the controlled and cross-line SciPlex3 tasks but \emph {not} on Frangieh, the only natural real dataset in the macro-mean, where mean/CMap cosine (0.600) beats energy (0.578). The macro-mean hides this counterexample; we state it here and in the text. \textbf {c}, Per-query regret reduction over \emph {all} 765 partial-observed queries: median $+0.118$, 72\% improved (the $+0.119$ quoted elsewhere is the 621-query gate-recommended subset, and the two are indistinguishable, which is panel \textbf {d}'s point). \textbf {d}, Recommended ($+0.119$) versus non-recommended ($+0.122$) subsets: the gate does not concentrate the gain. \textbf {e}, Alpha-crossover. The energy advantage does not close monotonically as the two subpopulations merge, and it does so in only one of the three cell lines (K562, from $1.00$ to $0.05$ in Hit@1 as $\alpha $ rises from $0.5$ to $0.9$); in A549 and MCF7 the gap is as large or larger at high $\alpha $. We report the per-line curves rather than a pooled trend for that reason. \textbf {f}, All five distributional metrics show positive Class-A regret reduction}{figure.caption.3}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Independent evaluation collapses the apparent advantage, and what survives it is mostly magnitude.} The metric-class ladder, read row by row. \textbf  {Row 1, Class A $\rightarrow $ Class B.} \textbf  {a}, One scorer, one query set, only the class of metric changes: a Class-A regret reduction of $+0.129$ becomes a Class-B MoA-nDCG gain of $-0.037$. \textbf  {b}, MoA-nDCG gain by cell line, uniformly at or below zero. \textbf  {c}, Minority-coverage gain by divergence quartile: significant (all Benjamini--Hochberg $q \approx 0$) and negligible, every bar $\leq 0.006$ on a metric whose values run 0.89 to 0.99. Bars are quartile means with s.e.m.; dashes mark the medians the text quotes. \textbf  {d}, The gate does not concentrate the advantage ($+0.119$ on 621 recommended queries versus $+0.122$ on 133 non-recommended). \textbf  {Row 2, why the gate cannot rescue it.} \textbf  {e}, The gate's structure-reliability axis is \emph  {anti}-correlated with true response divergence ($\rho =-0.21$), moving opposite to the quantity it is meant to track. \textbf  {f}, Recommended and non-recommended queries have indistinguishable divergence (Mann--Whitney $p=0.09$). \textbf  {g}, The real-data coverage advantage, one dot per task across all 239 tasks: real but negligible (overall mean $+0.0018$) and confined to the constructed cell-line mixtures ($+0.0043$ against $+0.0003$ in the natural data and $+0.00003$ under predicted candidates). We plot the difference rather than a dominance count; only 2 of the 10 threshold-crossing cross-line tasks survive seed resampling (circled). \textbf  {Row 3, Class C, an independent functional oracle.} 103\ leave-one-drug-out queries, SciPlex3 at 10\,$\mu $M. The oracle is \emph  {drug--drug functional similarity} measured out of context, not absolute potency (Methods). \textbf  {h}, Energy reaches $\rho =+0.276$ against $+0.083$ for the correctly specified mean incumbent, the first oracle-invisible criterion here on which the distributional score wins. It is not a \emph  {distributional} win: a query-dependent scalar matching only response \emph  {size} reaches $+0.232$ (hatched), and partialling that channel out leaves energy with $+0.097$ (dotted). The longest bar, potency matching at $+0.399$, is a diagnostic rather than a recommender, since it needs the query drug's own measured potency. Open circles, per-cell-line medians. \textbf  {i}, Per query, energy against that scalar: energy wins $62$ of 103\ queries, and the nominal $p=0.073$ is anticonservative because queries within a line share a candidate pool. The mismatched absolute-potency comparison is reported only as a confounder audit (Extended Data Fig.~5).}}{11}{figure.caption.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:4}{{4}{11}{\textbf {Independent evaluation collapses the apparent advantage, and what survives it is mostly magnitude.} The metric-class ladder, read row by row. \textbf {Row 1, Class A $\rightarrow $ Class B.} \textbf {a}, One scorer, one query set, only the class of metric changes: a Class-A regret reduction of $+0.129$ becomes a Class-B MoA-nDCG gain of $-0.037$. \textbf {b}, MoA-nDCG gain by cell line, uniformly at or below zero. \textbf {c}, Minority-coverage gain by divergence quartile: significant (all Benjamini--Hochberg $q \approx 0$) and negligible, every bar $\leq 0.006$ on a metric whose values run 0.89 to 0.99. Bars are quartile means with s.e.m.; dashes mark the medians the text quotes. \textbf {d}, The gate does not concentrate the advantage ($+0.119$ on 621 recommended queries versus $+0.122$ on 133 non-recommended). \textbf {Row 2, why the gate cannot rescue it.} \textbf {e}, The gate's structure-reliability axis is \emph {anti}-correlated with true response divergence ($\rho =-0.21$), moving opposite to the quantity it is meant to track. \textbf {f}, Recommended and non-recommended queries have indistinguishable divergence (Mann--Whitney $p=0.09$). \textbf {g}, The real-data coverage advantage, one dot per task across all 239 tasks: real but negligible (overall mean $+0.0018$) and confined to the constructed cell-line mixtures ($+0.0043$ against $+0.0003$ in the natural data and $+0.00003$ under predicted candidates). We plot the difference rather than a dominance count; only 2 of the 10 threshold-crossing cross-line tasks survive seed resampling (circled). \textbf {Row 3, Class C, an independent functional oracle.} \NCQUERIES \ leave-one-drug-out queries, SciPlex3 at 10\,$\mu $M. The oracle is \emph {drug--drug functional similarity} measured out of context, not absolute potency (Methods). \textbf {h}, Energy reaches $\rho =\ENERGYFUNC $ against $\MEANCTRLFUNC $ for the correctly specified mean incumbent, the first oracle-invisible criterion here on which the distributional score wins. It is not a \emph {distributional} win: a query-dependent scalar matching only response \emph {size} reaches $\MAGMATCHFUNC $ (hatched), and partialling that channel out leaves energy with $\ENERGYPARTIAL $ (dotted). The longest bar, potency matching at $\POTMATCHFUNC $, is a diagnostic rather than a recommender, since it needs the query drug's own measured potency. Open circles, per-cell-line medians. \textbf {i}, Per query, energy against that scalar: energy wins $\ENERGYWINS $ of \NCQUERIES \ queries, and the nominal $p=\ENERGYWINSP $ is anticonservative because queries within a line share a candidate pool. The mismatched absolute-potency comparison is reported only as a confounder audit (Extended Data Fig.~5)}{figure.caption.4}{}}
\citation{GDSC,GDSCLandscape}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {The statistical form of the criterion, not the biology, decides which method wins; and in real tissue both gates open without an advantage following.} \textbf  {Row 1.} \textbf  {a}, HIR-Bench's analytic flip boundary $\alpha ^*=B/(A+B)$ separates the regime in which the mean is a sufficient statistic from the one in which subpopulation structure can change the decision. \textbf  {b}, Circularity measured rather than argued: features a method can observe at query time reach AUC 0.788 against a 0.643 majority-class rate, whereas features derived from the benchmark's own latent utility matrix, of which the label is also a function, are at chance (0.400). Pseudo-replication inflates the circular set by $+0.240$ and the honest one by only $+0.048$. Effective $n$ is 28 parameter cells, not 13,440 (Methods). \textbf  {c}, The same effect between two external criteria built from the same proteins in the same cells, differing only in statistical form. \textbf  {The winner swaps}: the mean incumbent leads under the mean-shaped oracle ($+0.242$ against $+0.146$), the distributional score under the distribution-shaped one ($+0.529$ against $+0.334$), in each of the three immune conditions. Hatched, the magnitude-matching control, which compares no distributions: it climbs from $+0.125$ to $+0.352$ as the oracle turns distributional, yet energy still leads it by $+0.177$ there against $+0.022$ under the mean-shaped oracle. \textbf  {Row 2}, the two gates on ZhaoSims2021, ten glioblastoma patients (Methods). \textbf  {d}, Gate 2 posed as the same drug-response question in both settings (36 splits, 4 patients). Pooling drugs into classes holds the constructed ceiling at 0.700 (grey); one drug against one drug, the same cells give 0.879. All three arms are measured at the same granularity and under the same representation, so they are like for like; the 0.692 quoted for this construct elsewhere is the earlier in-fold run (Fig.~\ref {fig:6}e). In a real tumour the information is present (ceiling \textbf  {0.923}) and unsupervised clustering does not reach it (\textbf  {0.777}; median paired gap \textbf  {$+$0.117}, 95\% CI $[+0.115, +0.135]$ by patient-level bootstrap, against $+0.007$ constructed; $+0.122$ against $-0.002$ with no method selection anywhere; $+0.123$ $[+0.098, +0.138]$ without the dominant patient). \textbf  {Gate 2 in real tissue is an algorithmic bottleneck, not an informational one.} 30 of the 36 splits come from one patient and 39.9\% of cells fail the assignment margin, so both numbers are optimistic. \textbf  {e}, Gate 1: induced response cosine, median \textbf  {0.566} over 17 patient-drug pairs, against \textbf  {0.014--0.044} in our constructed mixtures. \textbf  {f}, The premise: over 18 within-patient drug pairs, mean-signature similarity ranks malignant-compartment response similarity at Spearman $\rho = \mathbf  {+0.878}$. \emph  {No $p$-value is given}, since 15 of the 18 pairs come from one patient. Both gates open here and the distributional advantage still does not appear: the two gates are necessary, not sufficient, and the condition that fails is the third one, decision relevance (Results).}}{15}{figure.caption.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:5}{{5}{15}{\textbf {The statistical form of the criterion, not the biology, decides which method wins; and in real tissue both gates open without an advantage following.} \textbf {Row 1.} \textbf {a}, HIR-Bench's analytic flip boundary $\alpha ^*=B/(A+B)$ separates the regime in which the mean is a sufficient statistic from the one in which subpopulation structure can change the decision. \textbf {b}, Circularity measured rather than argued: features a method can observe at query time reach AUC 0.788 against a 0.643 majority-class rate, whereas features derived from the benchmark's own latent utility matrix, of which the label is also a function, are at chance (0.400). Pseudo-replication inflates the circular set by $+0.240$ and the honest one by only $+0.048$. Effective $n$ is 28 parameter cells, not 13,440 (Methods). \textbf {c}, The same effect between two external criteria built from the same proteins in the same cells, differing only in statistical form. \textbf {The winner swaps}: the mean incumbent leads under the mean-shaped oracle ($+0.242$ against $+0.146$), the distributional score under the distribution-shaped one ($+0.529$ against $+0.334$), in each of the three immune conditions. Hatched, the magnitude-matching control, which compares no distributions: it climbs from $+0.125$ to $+0.352$ as the oracle turns distributional, yet energy still leads it by $+0.177$ there against $+0.022$ under the mean-shaped oracle. \textbf {Row 2}, the two gates on ZhaoSims2021, ten glioblastoma patients (Methods). \textbf {d}, Gate 2 posed as the same drug-response question in both settings (36 splits, 4 patients). Pooling drugs into classes holds the constructed ceiling at 0.700 (grey); one drug against one drug, the same cells give 0.879. All three arms are measured at the same granularity and under the same representation, so they are like for like; the 0.692 quoted for this construct elsewhere is the earlier in-fold run (Fig.~\ref {fig:6}e). In a real tumour the information is present (ceiling \textbf {0.923}) and unsupervised clustering does not reach it (\textbf {0.777}; median paired gap \textbf {$+$0.117}, 95\% CI $[+0.115, +0.135]$ by patient-level bootstrap, against $+0.007$ constructed; $+0.122$ against $-0.002$ with no method selection anywhere; $+0.123$ $[+0.098, +0.138]$ without the dominant patient). \textbf {Gate 2 in real tissue is an algorithmic bottleneck, not an informational one.} 30 of the 36 splits come from one patient and 39.9\% of cells fail the assignment margin, so both numbers are optimistic. \textbf {e}, Gate 1: induced response cosine, median \textbf {0.566} over 17 patient-drug pairs, against \textbf {0.014--0.044} in our constructed mixtures. \textbf {f}, The premise: over 18 within-patient drug pairs, mean-signature similarity ranks malignant-compartment response similarity at Spearman $\rho = \mathbf {+0.878}$. \emph {No $p$-value is given}, since 15 of the 18 pairs come from one patient. Both gates open here and the distributional advantage still does not appear: the two gates are necessary, not sufficient, and the condition that fails is the third one, decision relevance (Results)}{figure.caption.5}{}}
