\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {When can distributional information matter? Two measured gates, and a third condition they do not cover.} Candidate populations must show differential response (Gate 1) and that structure must be identifiable (Gate 2); neither asks whether the difference changes the decision (Gate 3). \textbf  {a}, The predict-then-rank design and the conditions a candidate population must satisfy: a held-out query is scored against candidate populations that are either observed or produced by a perturbation predictor. Gates 1 and 2 are measured in panels \textbf  {b}--\textbf  {f}. \textbf  {Gate 3 is drawn dashed because it is proposed here rather than tested as the other two are}; its evidence is Fig.~\ref {fig:5}f and Fig.~\ref {fig:4}h,i, and it is what panel \textbf  {g} is consistent with. \textbf  {b}, Predictor-specific retrieval gain (nDCG@10, distributional minus mean). The gain is small and \emph  {not} consistent in sign: $-0.071$ (nearest-neighbour), $-0.016$ (average-effect), $+0.027$ (latent-linear); one task, so no dispersion is available. \textbf  {c}, Gate 1. Predicted candidate populations \emph  {retain} baseline subpopulation structure (variance ratio 0.142 versus 0.138 for real ones, left axis) but do not reproduce \emph  {divergence}: the induced response cosine is 0.19--0.37 for predicted populations against 0.014 for real ones (right axis), where an additive predictor is pinned at exactly 1 by algebra. \textbf  {The predicted values here do not measure that ceiling and we do not interpret them}, because these populations are cross-context blends and a latent model's response against real controls also carries its reconstruction bias (Methods). \textbf  {d}, Structure diagnostics, predicted relative to real. \textbf  {e}, Gate 2 on a known bimodal mixture (K562, HDAC-class versus JAK-class cells), every method scored in one unit, best-permutation accuracy against the true labels. Unsupervised recovery is poor (best 0.674) and the \emph  {supervised ceiling} reaches only 0.692 (AUC 0.752), a gap of \textbf  {0.018}, so \emph  {within this benchmark} clustering already extracts what the representation holds. That reading is superseded: the ceiling is largely an artefact of \emph  {pooling} drugs into classes, and in a patient's tumour the same question gives 0.923 against 0.777 unsupervised, an algorithmic limit rather than an informational one (Fig.~\ref {fig:5}d). \textbf  {f}, The control that licenses reading panel \textbf  {e}: raising the separation between the two states makes the ceiling pull away from clustering as it should, so the absence of a gap at real separation is a measurement rather than an insensitive probe. \textbf  {g}, The conditional advantage a working information condition would produce is absent. Distributional-minus-mean MoA-nDCG gain by \emph  {true} response-divergence quartile (bars are means, error bars s.e.m.; Benjamini--Hochberg corrected). No stratum is positive and the lowest-divergence quartile is significantly \emph  {negative} (mean $-0.082$, $q = 5.8\times 10^{-5}$, $n = 166$). Where the theory predicts the largest gain there is none.}}{20}{figure.caption.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:6}{{6}{20}{\textbf {When can distributional information matter? Two measured gates, and a third condition they do not cover.} Candidate populations must show differential response (Gate 1) and that structure must be identifiable (Gate 2); neither asks whether the difference changes the decision (Gate 3). \textbf {a}, The predict-then-rank design and the conditions a candidate population must satisfy: a held-out query is scored against candidate populations that are either observed or produced by a perturbation predictor. Gates 1 and 2 are measured in panels \textbf {b}--\textbf {f}. \textbf {Gate 3 is drawn dashed because it is proposed here rather than tested as the other two are}; its evidence is Fig.~\ref {fig:5}f and Fig.~\ref {fig:4}h,i, and it is what panel \textbf {g} is consistent with. \textbf {b}, Predictor-specific retrieval gain (nDCG@10, distributional minus mean). The gain is small and \emph {not} consistent in sign: $-0.071$ (nearest-neighbour), $-0.016$ (average-effect), $+0.027$ (latent-linear); one task, so no dispersion is available. \textbf {c}, Gate 1. Predicted candidate populations \emph {retain} baseline subpopulation structure (variance ratio 0.142 versus 0.138 for real ones, left axis) but do not reproduce \emph {divergence}: the induced response cosine is 0.19--0.37 for predicted populations against 0.014 for real ones (right axis), where an additive predictor is pinned at exactly 1 by algebra. \textbf {The predicted values here do not measure that ceiling and we do not interpret them}, because these populations are cross-context blends and a latent model's response against real controls also carries its reconstruction bias (Methods). \textbf {d}, Structure diagnostics, predicted relative to real. \textbf {e}, Gate 2 on a known bimodal mixture (K562, HDAC-class versus JAK-class cells), every method scored in one unit, best-permutation accuracy against the true labels. Unsupervised recovery is poor (best 0.674) and the \emph {supervised ceiling} reaches only 0.692 (AUC 0.752), a gap of \textbf {0.018}, so \emph {within this benchmark} clustering already extracts what the representation holds. That reading is superseded: the ceiling is largely an artefact of \emph {pooling} drugs into classes, and in a patient's tumour the same question gives 0.923 against 0.777 unsupervised, an algorithmic limit rather than an informational one (Fig.~\ref {fig:5}d). \textbf {f}, The control that licenses reading panel \textbf {e}: raising the separation between the two states makes the ceiling pull away from clustering as it should, so the absence of a gap at real separation is a measurement rather than an insensitive probe. \textbf {g}, The conditional advantage a working information condition would produce is absent. Distributional-minus-mean MoA-nDCG gain by \emph {true} response-divergence quartile (bars are means, error bars s.e.m.; Benjamini--Hochberg corrected). No stratum is positive and the lowest-divergence quartile is significantly \emph {negative} (mean $-0.082$, $q = 5.8\times 10^{-5}$, $n = 166$). Where the theory predicts the largest gain there is none}{figure.caption.6}{}}
